From: nancy@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Date:     Mon, 9 May 94 12:58:49 GMT
Subject: [#94-5-108] ECON: The limits to comparative advantage

Marvin Minsky wrote: 
>
>>This is a priori, but I would think that low interest rates would
>>indicate a low time preference, and allow longer (and hopefully
>>more valuable) processes of production.
>>
>>I'm not saying that long processes are necessarily more valuable,
>>but that an economy that includes both them and the shorter
>>processes that would be all that exists at the higher interest
>>rates should be able to accomplish more.
>>
>>Nancy Lebovitz
>
>That sounds right -- and suggests that there is a serious bug in the
>standard concept of "THE interest rate".   Indeed, the more realistic
>variety is seen in the fatc that different duration bonds have different
>interest rates at different times.

I don't believe that there's a bug of that sort--as I understand it,
varying interest rates in one market just reflect different levels
of risk--THE interest rate is what the price would be for future
money that you were absolutely sure you'd get. All other interest
rates are lower but related to it.  (This just applies to commercial
loans--there may be lower or 0 rates for loans to friends and family.) 
>
>Perhaps this fiddling with a single 'prime rate' is just so constrained
>that it has mischievous consequences.  How about fiddling, instead, with
>the capital gain tax curves.   You can encourage N-year investments by
>holding the gain tax high for the first N years, etc.

The thing that has serious consequences is fiddling of any sort, imho.
One of the more interesting side effects which goes beyond the unintended
results of any particular dirigist effort is that more and more of
people's attention gets spent on understanding, changing, enforcing,
and evading the system. How many smart people who could have been
doing something useful are tax lawyers instead? 

As for fiddling with the capital gain tax curves, to the extent that
people are influenced by them, they'll be putting their money into
longer range investments than make sense, and neglecting useful
short range investments and/or losing the money.

What I was getting at was *not* that long-range investments are an
absolute good, but that if you (or your society) can afford them
as well as short-range ones, you'll be better off. The short-range
investments are important--they're the ones that take care of
immediate maintenance.

As I understand it, the fundamntal message of violence (and violence
includes the enforcement of laws) is "Pay attention to my judgement
of the best thing to do and ignore your own". While their are times
when other people's judgement is so bloody awful (i.e., they're
trying to kill you) that it makes sense to try using violence
to override it, for the most part I believe that everyone's
better off if people are free to apply their judgement to their
whole situation instead of to the person who's giving them orders.

Nancy Lebovitz

