From: pcm@world.std.com (Peter C. McCluskey)
Date: Mon, 9 May 1994 11:05:10 -0400
Subject: [#94-5-111] ECON: The limits to comparative advantage

 minsky@media.mit.edu  (Marvin Minsky) writes in X-Message-Number: #94-5-103:
>That sounds right -- and suggests that there is a serious bug in the
>standard concept of "THE interest rate".   Indeed, the more realistic
>variety is seen in the fatc that different duration bonds have different
>interest rates at different times.

 Abstraction is a serious bug??? Of course there are different risks
(and possibly different time preferences) associated with different
time periods. In the context of comparing post-singularity interest
rates with industrial age and pre-industrial age interest rates, it
makes sense to ignore short term fluctuations.

>Perhaps this fiddling with a single 'prime rate' is just so constrained
>that it has mischievous consequences.  How about fiddling, instead, with
>the capital gain tax curves.   You can encourage N-year investments by
>holding the gain tax high for the first N years, etc.

 I don't see the mischievous consequences you are talking about. I do see
some mischief in the belief that entity X has the wisdom to constructively
fiddle with the rewards to investment. People who are taking the actual
risks involved with loaning or borrowing money have more incentive to pick
the right period than the fiddlers do.
 It is certainly better to make investment decisions based on accurate
long-term forecasts than to be short-sighted, but that does not justify
the common sentimental preference for investments which require a long
time to mature. IBM's long term investments in mainframes haven't been
more useful than Compaq's short term investments in PC's. Xanadu's long
term investments in the best possible hypertext have helped fewer people
than quicker hacks such as WWW or Apple's Hypercard.
